/**
  @page guides-index General concepts
  @brief How meshes, finite element fields, weak forms, and solvers fit together.

  A Rodin application connects a geometric domain, a discrete field space,
  a weak problem and an algebraic solver. These guides develop those objects
  in that order and identify the C++ interfaces used at each stage.
  The @ref examples-index "examples and tutorials" supply complete numerical
  problems; the <a href="modules.html">module reference</a> describes
  individual APIs.

  @tableofcontents

  @section guides-learning-routes Choose a reading route

  | Task | Starting point | Continue with |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | Build and run a first solve | @ref guides-getting-started-installation | @ref guides-getting-started-first-problem |
  | Understand the finite element construction | @ref fem-ciarlet | @ref fem-spaces and @ref guides-weak-formulations |
  | Translate a model into C++ forms | @ref guides-variational | @ref guides-boundary-conditions and @ref guides-solvers |
  | Inspect a computed field | @ref guides-io | @ref gallery and @ref examples-index |
  | Move a local problem to a distributed backend | @ref guides-backends | @ref guides-mpi and @ref guides-petsc |

  @section guides-getting-started-index Start with a complete problem

  The introductory sequence assumes basic C++ and familiarity with calculus
  and linear algebra. Detailed finite element background is introduced in
  the guides below.

  1. @subpage guides-getting-started-installation — Configure the dependencies,
     build Rodin, and verify the installation.
  2. @subpage guides-getting-started-first-steps — Build a small program and
     identify the roles of a mesh, a finite element space, and symbolic fields.
  3. @subpage guides-getting-started-first-problem — Build and run a Poisson
     solve, then inspect the exported solution.
  4. @subpage guides-getting-started-core-concepts — Follow the complete path
     from geometric data to the assembled algebraic system.

  @section guides-geometry-index Describe the domain

  A mesh records both geometric coordinates and topological relations. These
  guides establish the distinction before introducing integration and fields.
  Incidence relations describe adjacency; geometric maps supply coordinates,
  Jacobians and measures for evaluation and integration.

  - @subpage guides-meshes — Create, load, inspect, and traverse a mesh.
  - @subpage guides-polytopes — Cells, faces, reference elements, and mappings.
  - @subpage guides-connectivity — Incidence relations between vertices,
    edges, faces, and cells.

  @section guides-fem-index Approximate fields on the mesh

  A finite element specifies local approximation and degrees of freedom;
  identifying compatible degrees of freedom across cells produces a global
  space. Field range, geometric mapping and coefficient ordering are separate
  choices in this construction.

  - @subpage fem-ciarlet — The finite element triple and local degrees of freedom.
  - @subpage fem-spaces — Scalar, vector, and complex spaces and grid functions.
  - @subpage guides-quadrature — Numerical integration, exactness, and curved cells.
  - @subpage guides-ordering — Component numbering, tensor indexing, and storage.

  @section guides-variational-index Express and solve equations

  The continuous weak problem determines the forms, boundary conditions, and
  algebraic properties that a solver must respect. Follow a derivation before
  selecting the corresponding form expressions, then check constraints,
  quadrature and solver status against that problem.

  - @subpage guides-weak-formulations — Derive weak forms from Poisson and
    elasticity equations.
  - @subpage guides-variational — Express bilinear and linear forms in C++.
  - @subpage guides-boundary-conditions — Essential, natural, periodic, and
    mixed boundary conditions.
  - @subpage guides-solvers — Choose and configure linear and nonlinear solvers.
  - @subpage guides-numerical-contracts — Signs, projection versus interpolation,
    constraints, quadrature, and solver lifetimes.
  - @subpage guides-interfaces-dg — Traces, facet integrals, jumps, and averages.

  @section guides-io-index Inspect and exchange results

  Mesh topology, geometric coordinates and field coefficients must be exported
  together with their interpretation. The I/O guides distinguish stored data,
  visualization output and time-dependent snapshots.

  - @subpage guides-io — Select mesh and field formats, export to ParaView,
    and write time series.

  @section guides-applications-index Build application models

  These guides combine the preceding concepts into fitting, design evolution
  and constitutive models. Their application examples expose geometry checks,
  objective histories and residual or derivative checks appropriate to each
  workflow.

  - @subpage guides-swift — Fit meshes to marked interfaces and inspect quality.
  - @subpage guides-shape-optimization — Shape derivatives, regularization,
    and boundary evolution.
  - @subpage guides-solid-mechanics — Finite-strain kinematics, constitutive
    laws, residuals, and consistent tangents.

  @section guides-parallel-index Choose an execution backend

  The local workflow provides the starting point. Parallel execution adds
  ownership, communication, and synchronization requirements. Establish the
  discrete problem locally before following the backend-specific construction
  and collective-operation rules.

  - @subpage guides-backends — Local, OpenMP, MPI, and PETSc execution paths.
  - @subpage guides-mpi — Distributed meshes, partitioning, and reconciliation.
  - @subpage guides-petsc — Distributed algebra, KSP/SNES, and multi-field problems.
  - @subpage guides-thread-safety — Shared state and synchronization requirements.

  @section guides-getting-started-help Further reference

  The @ref notation-index "notation reference" defines mathematical symbols.
  The <a href="modules.html">module reference</a> provides API declarations,
  and the [project repository](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin) hosts
  source code and issue discussions.
*/
